      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "After the 1806 Cherokee treaty, settlers from East Tennessee began clearing the Elk River valley in February 1807.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        29,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/prospect_giles_county_tennessee",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "James Ford arrived June 4, 1807, and built a house near Ford’s Creek, a tributary that still runs through Prospect.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/prospect_giles_county_tennessee",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Bethel Masonic Lodge, erected in 1850, served as a church, school, and Masonic hall, reflecting the community’s early civic life.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/prospect_giles_county_tennessee",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Prospect ranks among the first permanently settled spots in Giles County, with pioneers establishing farms as early as 1807.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/prospect_giles_county_tennessee",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Cully Alton Cobb, born in Prospect in 1884, later became a leading agricultural educator and editor of the Progressive Farmer.",
